// momovec - a vector rasteriser: normalized paths in, pixels out.
//
// Ported from the vector study, which distilled it out of a TypeScript port of
// Alois Zingl's Bresenham curve algorithms. The study is the reference this is
// held against, number for number - see its PORT.md and NOTES.md.
//
// Three things about the shape of this library are worth knowing before reading
// any of it.
//
// **It has no cubic.** Zingl's cubic segment routine is genuinely floating point -
// a square root for its resolution factor, divisions by 8 and 64 that do not
// divide - so unlike the quadratic it cannot be ported by answering a range
// question. Cubics are converted to chains of quadratics by the study, before the
// data gets here, and nothing in this directory knows they ever existed. That
// deletes a rasteriser, two splitters, the float split points and a subdivision
// stack; it costs 30% more stored data.
//
// **Every segment that arrives is already limited**, meaning its control point
// lies inside its endpoints' bounding box, and no wider than 15 pixels. Both are
// guaranteed by the study, which splits at the gradient extremes and subdivides to
// the audited range before rounding. 15 is measured: above it the quadratic's
// error terms leave a signed 16-bit word.
//
// **Coordinates are read through `mapX( at )` and `mapY( at )`**, never out of the
// arrays directly, and a program picks which pair it gets: `momovec/direct.momo`
// reads the point as stored, `momovec/zoom.momo` scales it about a centre as it is
// read. Two files defining the same names, one of them included - the same
// arrangement as `subdiv.momo` against `quadflat.momo`.
//
// They are parameterised consts, so the body substitutes inline and the direct pair
// compiles to exactly the array index it replaced: measured, and the instructions in
// all ten geometry projects are byte-identical either way. Taking the INDEX rather
// than the coordinate is what makes a transform possible at all - there is nothing
// to transform twice, and nowhere to forget.
//
// **It calls routines it does not define.** `plot( x, y )` is supplied by the
// PROGRAM, and so are the scene arrays. Momo splices every include into one
// namespace and resolves routines by name whatever file declared them, so a
// library may call forward into its caller and the call compiles to a direct
// `call` with no indirection. That is how one rasteriser serves a harness that
// prints numbers and a demo that writes VRAM, with no runtime cost and without
// waiting on DESIGN 19 or 21.
//
// What a program must supply:
//
//   sub plot( i16 x, i16 y )        where a pixel goes
//   const u8[]  opKind              0 move, 1 line, 2 quad
//   const i16[] px, py              points, 1, 1 or 2 per op
//   const u16[] pathOpStart, pathOpCount, pathPointStart

const screenW = 320
const screenH = 200

// Coordinates are i16 and not u16, deliberately. A control point clipped at the
// edge of the canvas goes negative, and a step direction is signed by
// construction - so DESIGN 4's rule that u16 does not mix with signed types means
// picking one and staying there. Signed is the one that can represent the inputs.
const i16 xMin = 0
const i16 yMin = 0
const i16 xMax = screenW - 1
const i16 yMax = screenH - 1

// The coordinate bound, DERIVED rather than chosen, and shared by two routines that
// arrive at it independently:
//
//   drawQuadAny's midpoint ladder computes `(a + b) / 2`, and a midpoint never leaves
//   the range of its inputs - so the largest intermediate at any depth is twice a
//   coordinate, and it has to fit a signed word.
//
//   drawLine computes `iabs( x1 - x0 )` as an i16, so the difference between two
//   coordinates has to fit one too.
//
// Both give 16,383. The study replayed the ladder at each candidate to pin it: at
// 16,383 the worst intermediate is 32,766 and fits; at 16,384 it is 32,768 and does
// not.
//
// **This was documented as 8,191 and unenforced, which was wrong twice over.** The
// figure came with the words "leaves a factor of four spare" - caution rather than a
// derivation, rejecting legal input for no reason - and nothing on the target checked
// it, so a caller who exceeded it got wrapped arithmetic and a silently wrong drawing.
// That is the failure mode this port has been bitten by twice, and the only remaining
// place it could happen.
const i16 maxCoord = 16383

// Set when geometry outside the bound is handed to the rasteriser.
//
// **Nothing is drawn in that case, deliberately.** A chord is no safer - drawLine has
// the same bound - and clamping would put a plausible wrong shape on the screen. A
// missing shape plus an explicit marker is the only unambiguous answer. Same argument
// as `crossingsOverflowed` and `subdivOverflowed`.
bool coordOutOfRange

bool inCoordRange( i16 v ) {
  if ( v > maxCoord ) return false
  if ( v < -maxCoord ) return false

  return true
}
